// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Opportunities & Competitions — the catalog expansion.
//
// src/data/opportunities.js holds the original curated set and the schema; this
// file is what turned it from a list into a database. Same integrity bar,
// restated because it binds harder at this size:
//
//   - Every entry is a real, independently verifiable program. No invented
//     names, no invented organisations.
//   - Deliberately NO `url` field, exactly as in the base file. Most of what is
//     below varies by state, chapter, hospital or campus, and one hardcoded
//     link is how a student ends up on the wrong city's page. Where we DO know
//     a program's own URL and its real deadline, it gets a row in
//     src/data/opportunityPrograms.js instead — that is the whole reason the
//     two files exist separately.
//   - `eligibility` is a general description, never a promise. Where a rule is
//     the thing that catches people out (an age floor, a residency rule, a
//     nomination you cannot make yourself), it is said plainly rather than
//     softened.
//   - No pay-to-attend "youth leadership summit" products. The two that are
//     worth naming are named in the structured layer's honest-about-cost tier,
//     where the warning is the point.
//
// ── What this expansion is weighted toward, and why ─────────────────────────
// Small. Regional. Free. The programs a student has heard of are the ones with
// the worst odds, and a database that only lists those is a database that tells
// every student in the country to apply to the same eight things. Most of what
// follows is open to one state, one city, or one hospital's catchment — which
// is precisely what makes it winnable, and precisely why nobody blogs about it.
//
// Two categories here are, per hour of effort, the highest-value things in the
// entire app and almost nobody knows they exist:
//   1. Free public residential STEM high schools (NCSSM, IMSA, TAMS and their
//      dozen siblings). Two years of university-level science, paid for by your
//      own state government.
//   2. Hospital- and university-run pipeline programs aimed explicitly at
//      students who do not already have a scientist in the family.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

